From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Taozuhong <taozuhong@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:22:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] MinGW: IPC shared memory and process launch support

IPC and process plumbing for the GTK/MinGW target:
- WebCore SharedMemory: add the Win32 file-mapping implementation
  (platform/win/SharedMemoryWin.cpp) and register it in WebCore
  SourcesGTK.txt next to SharedMemoryUnix.cpp.
- IPC: loopback UDP datagram transport emulating SOCK_SEQPACKET with a
  credential handshake, HANDLE attachment passing via DuplicateHandle,
  process-launch port bootstrap and .exe path resolution, FenceMonitor
  guards (experimental design, see BUILD-NOTES).

  Update 22 (fixing the round-22 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 WebCore failures):
  SharedMemory.h kept the upstream split where the private storage picks
  the USE(UNIX_DOMAIN_SOCKETS) branch first (UnixFileDescriptor on
  MinGW) while the inline HANDLE handle() accessor keyed on plain
  OS(WINDOWS) and referenced the Win32Handle member m_handle, which the
  MinGW configuration never declares - "use of undeclared identifier
  'm_handle'" at SharedMemory.h:142. The accessor now takes the same
  branch order as the storage: under USE(UNIX_DOMAIN_SOCKETS) &&
  OS(WINDOWS) it reconstructs the mapping handle from
  m_fileDescriptor's int payload via WTF::handleFromIntValue(); the
  plain-OS(WINDOWS) branch is kept for the non-USE configuration.
  SharedMemoryWin.cpp was written against the non-USE storage layout
  (Win32Handle m_handle members, Handle(Win32Handle{...}) ctor) that
  the MinGW configuration never compiles, and it would also have
  collided at link time with SharedMemoryUnix.cpp, whose
  USE(UNIX_DOMAIN_SOCKETS) guard (without !OS(WINDOWS)) compiles on
  MinGW and cannot build there at all (shm_open is not part of the
  mingw-compat mman subset). SharedMemoryUnix.cpp is now excluded under
  OS(WINDOWS), and SharedMemoryWin.cpp is rewritten to implement the
  exact Unix-flavored API surface (SharedMemoryHandle copy ctor and
  releaseHandle, SharedMemory::allocate/map/wrapMap/destructor/
  createHandle) on CreateFileMappingW/MapViewOfFile, storing the
  mapping handle in the UnixFileDescriptor int payload; the Unix
  version's runtime behavior is mirrored (CopyOnWrite rejected,
  m_isWrappingMap release semantics, DuplicateHandle-based
  createHandle).

  Update 23 (fixing the round-23 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 WebCore failure and
  pre-empting the same class of failure in the WebKit layer): gio declares
  and implements the unix fd-list API (GUnixFDList,
  g_dbus_proxy_call_with_unix_fd_list*) only for G_OS_UNIX builds - on
  Windows gdbusproxy.h keeps the declarations inside #ifdef G_OS_UNIX
  (hence "use of undeclared identifier
  'g_dbus_proxy_call_with_unix_fd_list_sync'" at DesktopPortal.cpp:180/280)
  while gunixfdlist.h still ships declarations without any implementation
  in the Windows gio DLL, so other users would only fail at link time.
  DesktopPortal.cpp now stubs the two openPipewireRemote bodies on
  Windows (the freedesktop portals and PipeWire fd passing do not exist
  there; all callers already handle the empty result as
  PermissionDenied), keeping the unified-source grouping untouched.
  The same gio-unix pattern is pre-empted in three WebKit-layer files
  that compile after WebCore: ArgumentCodersGLib encodes the empty
  fd-list case on Windows (wire format unchanged; no GUnixFDList can
  exist there) and decodes while keeping the stream in sync; the
  WebKitUserMessage fd-list property falls back to G_TYPE_OBJECT /
  G_IS_OBJECT checks so the public API entry points keep working with a
  NULL fd-list; the WebKit print-portal path fails gracefully instead
  of referencing g_unix_fd_list_* symbols. HAVE(GDESKTOPAPPINFO) in
  NotificationService.cpp and the glib-unix.h include in
  AuxiliaryProcessMain.cpp were audited and are already safe.

  Update 24 (fixing the round-24 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 WebCore failure and
  pre-empting the same collision in the remaining WebCore TUs): the
  WebCore prefix header (WebCorePrefix.h, force-included as the PCH of
  every WebCore TU) includes <windows.h> under PLATFORM(WIN); with
  WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN undefined MinGW's windows.h pulls in rpc.h, whose
  "#define interface struct" MIDL compatibility macro then breaks any TU
  that uses "interface" as an identifier - "invalid parameter name:
  'struct' is a keyword" at WebKitAutoconfigProxyResolver.cpp:100
  followed by three "declaration of anonymous struct must be a
  definition" errors in the GProxyResolverInterface vtable initializer.
  A whole-tree sweep for bare "interface" identifiers in GTK-compiled
  sources found the same construct in the accessibility/atspi backend
  (AccessibilityAtspi.h/.cpp, AccessibilityObjectCollectionAtspi.cpp -
  local declarations, function parameters, WTF::map lambdas and
  range-for loop variables), which compiles in later unified batches
  and would fail next; every bare "interface" identifier is renamed to
  "iface" (WebKit's conventional GInterface-init parameter name), no
  functional change. Verified in the sandbox against the MSYS2
  clang64/clangarm64 header sets: the pristine TU reproduces the CI
  error set line-for-line, the renamed TU compiles clean, and JSC's
  CommonIdentifiers.h macro(interface) usage is safe because every use
  site stringizes (#name) or token-pastes (name##Keyword). WebKit-layer
  candidates were audited and need no change: NetworkMDNSRegisterGLib
  compiles only under ENABLE(WEB_RTC) (off), NetworkRTCMonitor only
  under USE(LIBWEBRTC) (off), PointerLockManagerWayland only under
  PLATFORM(WAYLAND) (off); FontPlatformData.h's "interface IDWriteFont"
  forward declarations live under USE(DIRECTWRITE) (off) and expand to
  valid struct declarations regardless.

  Update 25 (fixing the round-25 CLANGARM64 WebKit PCH failure):
  IPC::Semaphore's OS(WINDOWS) section declares
  "Win32Handle win32Handle() const { return Win32Handle { m_semaphoreHandle }; }"
  but Win32Handle is move-only (its user-declared move constructor deletes
  the implicit copy constructor), so brace-initializing from the lvalue
  member selects the deleted copy constructor and every WebKit PCH that
  includes IPCSemaphore.h dies with "call to implicitly-deleted copy
  constructor of 'Win32Handle'" at IPCSemaphore.h:77 - CI hit it in all
  four PCH targets (WebKit, WebKitUIProcess, WebKitWebProcess,
  WebKitGPUProcess) because WebKitPrefix.h pulls the header in
  unconditionally. The accessor has no in-tree callers (it is the Windows
  counterpart of the Cocoa Semaphore::createSendRight() above, which mints
  a new send right for cross-process duplication), so it now returns
  m_semaphoreHandle.duplicate() - an owning DuplicateHandle copy matching
  the name's implied semantics. Sandbox: the pristine TU reproduces the CI
  error line-for-line on x86_64-w64-mingw32 and aarch64-w64-mingw32 with
  real MSYS2 headers; the duplicate() form compiles clean on both.

  Update 26 (fixing the round-26 CLANGARM64 WebKit unified-source
  failures, first hit at UnifiedSource-UIProcess-8/9.cpp): WKAPICast.h's
  tail includes WKAPICastWin.h whenever WIN32/_WIN32 is defined, and
  both are compiler builtins on every MinGW target. The file ships only
  in the Windows CA port tree (UIProcess/API/C/win/), which the GTK
  release tarball strips, so every WebKit TU including WKAPICast.h died
  with "fatal error: 'WKAPICastWin.h' file not found" at WKAPICast.h:444
  as soon as the round-25 PCH fixes let the unified sources compile.
  Skip the include when BUILDING_GTK__ is defined: OptionsGTK.cmake
  defines it globally for every GTK-port target, the GTK cast table
  (WKAPICastGtk.h, included just above) already maps WKViewRef to
  WebKitWebViewBase, and the Win header's sole content -
  WK_ADD_API_MAPPING(WKViewRef, WebView) for the CA port's WebView
  class - has no GTK-port consumer; including both would conflict, as
  both headers define toAPI<>/toImpl<> specializations for WKViewRef.
  Ports that do not define BUILDING_GTK__ keep upstream behavior.

  Update 27 (fixing the round-27 CLANG64 WebKit IPC unified-source
  failures, first hit at UnifiedSource-Platform-2/4.cpp [8192/8564]):
  the previous rounds' Windows IPC plumbing was flavor-inconsistent -
  Attachment.h kept the upstream OS(WINDOWS) HANDLE-based struct while
  the GTK port's SourcesGTK.txt manifest compiles the unix-flavored
  IPC sources (glib/ConnectionGLib.cpp, unix/ArgumentCodersUnix.cpp,
  unix/IPCSemaphoreUnix.cpp, unix/IPCUtilitiesUnix.cpp) against
  USE(UNIX_DOMAIN_SOCKETS)-selected flavors of Connection.h
  Identifier, ConnectionHandle and SharedMemoryHandle (Type =
  UnixFileDescriptor, whose MinGW payload carries packed Win32 handles
  since Update 22). Every mixed-style expression failed to compile:
  UnixMessage.h:160 Vector<Attachment>::append(UnixFileDescriptor),
  ArgumentCodersUnix.cpp:37/42/48 Attachment conversions, Connection.h
  Identifier{HANDLE}, ConnectionHandle{Win32Handle},
  SharedMemory::Handle{Win32Handle}, ConnectionGLib.cpp:124
  intptr_t(identifier.handle), :441 GUniquePtr::outPtr (2.54's
  GUniquePtr is a plain std::unique_ptr alias; only GRefPtr has
  outPtr()), :562 UniqueRef::releaseToUniquePtr (the method is named
  moveToUniquePtr) and :769 Identifier/ConnectionHandle brace
  initialization. The fix completes the unix-flavor unification for
  the GLib port on Windows instead of maintaining two styles:
  (a) Attachment.h, IPCSemaphore.h include/class/member sections and
  the IPCSemaphore.serialization.in coder accessors now select the
  Windows CA-port flavors only under OS(WINDOWS) &&
  !USE(UNIX_DOMAIN_SOCKETS), so MinGW GTK falls through to the unix
  flavors (Attachment = UnixFileDescriptor; Semaphore carries
  UnixFileDescriptor m_fd) while pure-Win port builds keep upstream
  behavior; the generated Semaphore coder then encodes via
  duplicateDescriptor() -> ArgumentCoder<UnixFileDescriptor> ->
  Attachment, which rides the Winsock/HANDLE value in its payload.
  (b) unix/IPCSemaphoreUnix.cpp gains the OS(WINDOWS) branches for the
  real semaphore behavior - CreateSemaphoreW in the constructor,
  ReleaseSemaphore in signal(), WaitForSingleObject (INFINITE /
  deadline-milliseconds clamped at 0) in wait()/waitFor() - because
  with the class shape unified to m_fd the upstream Linux-only bodies
  would otherwise compile to silent no-ops on Windows.
  (c) glib/ConnectionGLib.cpp Windows sections are rewritten to the
  unix-flavored types: platformInitialize drops the
  static_cast<intptr_t>(identifier.handle) branch in favor of the
  upstream identifier.handle.release() (the packed winsock SOCKET is
  exactly what g_socket_new_from_fd() expects on Windows); the
  createMessageDecoder/sendOutputMessage attachment plumbing packs and
  unpacks Attachment::Adopt / UnixFileDescriptor::Adopt values via
  WTF::intFromHandleValue / WTF::handleFromIntValue instead of
  Win32Handle wrappers; createConnectionIdentifierPair returns
  Identifier{UnixFileDescriptor{...}} / ConnectionHandle{...}; the
  ArgumentCoder<Win32Handle> definitions now mint attachments from
  WTF::intFromHandleValue(handle.release()) and decode through
  attachment->release() so the UnixFileDescriptor destructor does not
  close the handle the returned Win32Handle adopts (fixing a latent
  double-close in the previous HANDLE-struct form); the deferred
  output queue uses encoder.moveToUniquePtr(); the Windows
  readyReadHandler keeps its peer-address capture on a GRefPtr
  (outPtr()-capable) instead of the alias GUniquePtr.
  Sandbox: a pristine round-26 baseline tree reproduced the CI error
  set line-for-line (UnifiedSource-Platform-2/4 sites at
  Vector.h:1537/1575, ArgumentCodersUnix.cpp:37/42/48,
  IPCSemaphoreUnix.cpp:47/48/126/128/133 and the six
  ConnectionGLib.cpp sites) with clang 19 targeting
  x86_64-w64-mingw32; after the fix all four TUs compile with 0 errors
  on x86_64-w64-mingw32 and aarch64-w64-mingw32, and the platform-
  generic Encoder.cpp/IPCUtilities.cpp stay clean.

  Update 28 (fixing the round-28 CLANG64 failures that remain after the
  Update 27 unix-flavor unification, first hit at
  UnifiedSource-Shared-24/25.cpp, WebKitUIProcess's WebKitPrivate.cpp and
  WebKitProtocolHandler.cpp; ninja stops after four failed edges, so the
  Platform buckets from the previous round never got a chance to run):
  (a) GtkVersioning.h's GTK4 branch defined GTK3-signature compatibility
  wrappers named gtk_init(int*, char***)/gtk_init_check(int*, char***)
  whose bodies call the zero-argument GTK4 entry points. GTK 4.16 (MSYS2
  ships 4.18) turned both into function-like macros
  (gtk_init_abi_check/gtk_init_check_abi_check, gtkmain.h:78-79), so the
  wrapper definition lines themselves get macro-expanded and every TU
  that includes the header dies with "too many arguments provided to
  function-like macro invocation" plus seven cascade errors (8 errors per
  TU on CI). The wrappers now exist only when gtk_init_check is still a
  plain symbol (GTK < 4.16, where they legally overload the C
  prototypes); with the macros present the same GTK3-signature entry
  points move to webkitGtkInit()/webkitGtkInitCheck(), whose bodies call
  the zero-argument forms and let them expand through the macros.
  Display.cpp's gtk_init_check() call site follows the same switch; the
  only other GTK4-path callers (MiniBrowser main.c, Tools/TestWebKitAPI)
  call gtk_init() with no arguments and keep working as-is (the API tests
  are not built).
  (b) AuxiliaryProcessMain.cpp:110 still constructed the connection
  identifier straight from the winsock SOCKET as
  Identifier{reinterpret_cast<HANDLE>(ipcSocket)} - a leftover of the
  pre-Update-27 HANDLE flavor with no matching constructor (candidates
  take UnixFileDescriptor&&/ConnectionHandle&&, CI error at
  AuxiliaryProcessMain.cpp:110). It now rides the same unix-flavored
  carrier as the rest of the IPC:
  Identifier{UnixFileDescriptor{WTF::intFromHandleValue(socket), Adopt}},
  identical in shape to Update 27's createConnectionIdentifierPair
  construction that the sandbox already compiles cleanly.
  (c) WebKitProtocolHandler.cpp:761 called renderBufferDescription() under
  #if PLATFORM(GTK) while the function's definition sits inside the
  #if USE(LIBDRM) block of the same file - an upstream latent bug that
  Linux GTK never sees (USE_LIBDRM is always on there) and that our
  -DUSE_LIBDRM=OFF exposes as "use of undeclared identifier
  'renderBufferDescription'". The call site now requires USE(LIBDRM) as
  well; the hardware-acceleration info page simply omits the buffer-format
  row when LIBDRM is disabled.
  (d) WebKitPrefix.h now includes <winsock2.h> immediately after
  <wtf/Platform.h> on OS(WINDOWS): the prefix header pulls windows.h into
  every WebKit TU through the WTF headers, so the IPC layer's own
  #include <winsock2.h> tripped mingw-w64's "Please include winsock2.h
  before windows.h" #warning (CI noise at AuxiliaryProcessMain.cpp:54 and
  ConnectionGLib.cpp:51). Pinning the order in the prefix header silences
  the warning for the whole target.
  Verification: clang 19 --target=x86_64-w64-mingw32 against real MSYS2
  headers reproduces the winsock2 #warning before the fix and none after;
  the GtkVersioning.h switch is preprocessed in both GTK states (macros
  present/absent) with mock GTK4 prototypes, and the (b) construction is
  byte-identical in shape to the Update 27 createConnectionIdentifierPair
  line already verified in the round-27 sandbox on both mingw targets.

  Update 29 (fixing the round-29 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 realpath errors):
  WebKitWebContext.cpp's sandbox-path helper
  pathExistsAndIsNotHomeDirectory() calls realpath(3), which MinGW does
  not provide - "use of undeclared identifier 'realpath'" at :1427 and
  :1439 on both clang runners (upstream only ever built this TU on
  Linux; it feeds webkit_web_context_add_path_to_sandbox()). The
  OS(WINDOWS) branch replaces the call with mingwRealpathForSandbox():
  MultiByteToWideChar(CP_UTF8) -> GetFullPathNameW ->
  WideCharToMultiByte(CP_UTF8) round trip, i.e. a canonical absolute
  path malloc'd exactly like realpath(path, nullptr) with UTF-8
  preserved for the g_get_home_dir() comparison, plus g_file_test()
  restoring realpath(3)'s ENOENT-for-missing-path semantics so the
  caller keeps its "must be created before adding it to the sandbox"
  diagnostic. The block pins its own include order (winsock2.h before
  windows.h, matching the WebKitPrefix.h Update 28 pin, and NOMINMAX)
  because windows.h visibility differs between the WebKit and
  WebKitUIProcess prefix-header flavors; the "/home" special case is
  dropped in the Windows branch as a Linux bwrap artifact. Verification:
  clang 19 --target=x86_64-w64-mingw32 against mingw-w64 headers
  reproduces the two undeclared-realpath errors with the original
  shape, compiles the patched shape with zero errors and no min/max
  macro leakage, tolerates a pre-included windows.h, and the kernel32
  import library exposes GetFullPathNameW/MultiByteToWideChar/
  WideCharToMultiByte.

  Update 31 (the round-31 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 compile errors in the same
  shim): WebKit 2.54 moved WTF::Vector::data() into the private section
  (Vector.h:985; the public buffer accessors are span() and
  mutableSpan()), so the six Win32 calls inside mingwRealpathForSandbox()
  - two MultiByteToWideChar output buffers, two GetFullPathNameW
  arguments, two WideCharToMultiByte arguments - fail with "'data' is a
  private member of 'WTF::Vector<wchar_t>'" (6 errors on CLANG64,
  mirrored on CLANGARM64). All six call sites now go through
  mutableSpan().data(), whose std::span::data() is public and hands the
  exact same pointer to the same arguments; no length or ownership
  changes.
